Memo: Voxel Engine Development Status & Roadmap

To: Development Team

Subject: Status Report and Strategic Roadmap for Isometric Voxel Engine

This memo summarizes our current development progress, technical challenges, and the architectural roadmap for the isometric voxel engine.
1. Current Status & Core Progress

    Isometric Depth Sorting: We have moved away from manual z_index manipulation (which caused rendering conflicts) toward a robust coordinate-based system. We now use a standardized depth calculation formula (screen_pos.y + (z * VOXEL_HEIGHT)) across both ChunkView and Player scripts to ensure consistent 2.5D sorting.

    Vertical Movement: The player jump mechanics have been refactored. We have replaced the dual-system (separate visual_z and voxel_position.z) with a unified vertical state model. This removes previous "ghosting" effects, synchronizes physics with animation, and allows for predictable ground/collision detection.

    Rendering Architecture: We are successfully using MultiMeshInstance3D with QuadMesh for world geometry to maximize performance. We have rejected switching to BoxMesh as it introduces unnecessary vertex complexity without solving occlusion requirements.

2. Technical Challenges & Known Issues

    Occlusion & Z-Fighting: Even with unified depth math, the player occasionally renders behind voxels of the same height because the GPU ignores the depth buffer due to no_depth_test = true. We are relying on sorting_offset to handle sorting, which is efficient but requires perfect mathematical parity between the player and static chunks.

    Main Thread Stuttering: High overhead during chunk loading persists due to synchronous mesh generation in the main thread.

    Input/Rotation Sensitivity: The camera and input rotation logic has suffered from rubber-banding and inverted directions following camera state changes.

3. Recommended Roadmap & Next Steps

    Mesh Optimization (High Priority): Offload heavy mesh generation (build_height_map, build_top_surface) to background worker threads. The main thread should only handle the final assignment of pre-calculated mesh data to the renderer.

    Refine Occlusion: * Transition away from no_depth_test = true where possible.

        Implement Alpha Scissor or a Depth Pre-pass in materials to allow the GPU to handle true occlusion clipping.

    Standardize Sorting: * Ensure all depth keys are rounded to integers before assignment to sorting_offset to match the discrete nature of the chunk rendering system.

        Strictly enforce the use of the IsoMath.iso_depth() method across all objects to prevent "magic number" drift.

    Movement Polish: Implement variable jump height (button-hold duration) and coyote-time to enhance player responsiveness.

Summary: We have established a stable foundation for isometric depth sorting and player physics. Our next major objective is to transition heavy-lifting tasks to worker threads to eliminate frame-rate stutters during streaming, followed by a transition to GPU-native occlusion to resolve the remaining sorting edge cases.

