Executive Summary

The game is an action-oriented roguelike centered around a spreading crystalline corruption that behaves like a fluid. Players start near the crystal spawn, flee to power up via items/relics, then fight back through an increasingly overtaken world for an endurance challenge before a biome-influenced boss fight. The world is finite (non-infinite) to create meaningful tension, lose conditions, and strategic map control. Core loop emphasizes mobility, terrain manipulation (placing walls), and dynamic biome interactions. Technical foundation has shifted to a performant 3D voxel engine.
Core Game Vision & Mechanics

    Player Role: Single character who can place walls/structures to shape terrain and control crystal flow.
    Crystal Spread: Acts as a fluid — pools in low areas, slowed by high ground. Spread rate and combat style vary heavily by biome:
        Mountain: Slower initial spread but creates large crystal “lakes”/walls later.
        Desert: Fast spread, leads to more enemy encounters rather than massive crystal barriers.
    Progression Loop: Run away → gather power (items, relics) → fight back to origin for boss. ~10-minute endurance phase through heavily corrupted map before boss.
    Lose Conditions:
        Crystal consumes too much safe ground (overtake).
        Player death (primary fun/stakes loss).
    Win Condition: Defeat the boss at the crystal’s origin after fighting through the spread.
    Roguelike Elements: Procedural generation (seed-based terrain + RNG for structures, towns, items, mobs, trees, chests). Player power via items/relics. Runs can include saves/loads.
    World Features:
        Distinct biomes with unique height/terrain traits (ravines, canyons with rivers, valleys with lakes, basins, plateaus).
        Caves as baked-in setpieces/dungeons.
        Potential protectable elements (e.g., cities/towns) under discussion but not core.

Technical Progress (as of late April 2026)

    Engine & Language: Moved away from Python (performance issues with 2.5D) → Fully 3D voxel engine with vector3 coordinates.
    Major Optimizations Implemented:
        Greedy meshing (groups faces, preserves types, huge render time reduction).
        Face culling (only render visible faces).
        Chunk-level texture batching.
        Atlas shader per cube face (top/side/bottom).
        Biome-based block textures.
        Smooth lighting (ambient + face shading).
        Debug UI (matches internal data, shows player pos, chunk ID, tile name).
        Camera smoothing/trailing.
        Player occlusion (hides behind walls/corners).
    World Generation: Finite map size (whole world can generate at once). Seed-based terrain consistency + layered RNG for content.
    Other Notes: Chunk manager/renderer discussions resolved toward performance-first approach. Version 0.2.3 pushed with core rendering improvements.

Key Decisions & Open Items

Decided:

    Finite world with active lose condition (crystal spread + time pressure).
    Biome-driven gameplay variety (no need to hard-program some behaviors — they emerge naturally).
    Focus on mobility over static defense.
    3D voxel tech path.

Under Discussion / Parking Lot:

    Exact boss mechanics and “awakening” scene.
    Depth of protectable settlements/cities.
    Respawn rules and death-rush prevention.
    Save/load implementation details.
    Infinite world temptation vs. finite scope.

Challenges & Risks

    Balancing crystal spread speed vs. player power curve.
    Performance at high crystal coverage (many dynamic blocks).
    Ensuring biomes feel meaningfully different in both exploration and late-game combat.
    Scope control — avoiding feature creep on towns/structures while keeping runs engaging.

Strategic Direction & Next Steps

    Immediate:
        Finalize map size parameters and crystal fluid simulation.
        Prototype biome height features and crystal interaction.
        Implement basic item/relic system and wall placement.
    Short-Term:
        Full vertical slice: Start → escape → return fight with basic boss.
        Polish voxel rendering and add procedural structures (trees, chests, simple towns).
        Add save/load system.
    Medium-Term:
        Biome-specific enemy/mob behaviors.
        Dungeon/cave setpieces.
        Balancing passes and playtesting.

Success Metrics: Engaging crystal-terrain interaction, strong roguelike replayability, smooth 60+ FPS performance, and clear “runaway then push back” fantasy.

